1
00:00:00,530 --> 00:00:03,530
The final section of chapter one of your text

2
00:00:03,530 --> 00:00:06,030
is a discussion of amyloid.

3
00:00:06,030 --> 00:00:09,530
Amyloid is important to discuss in this particular sequence

4
00:00:09,530 --> 00:00:12,530
because it is another mechanism

5
00:00:12,530 --> 00:00:15,530
by which cells can become injured,

6
00:00:15,530 --> 00:00:18,530
and we've obviously been talking about cellular injury

7
00:00:18,530 --> 00:00:21,530
and cell death, and amyloid is one way

8
00:00:21,530 --> 00:00:24,530
by which cells can become injured and eventually die

9
00:00:24,530 --> 00:00:27,530
because amyloid has the ability to damage tissues.

10
00:00:27,530 --> 00:00:28,530
Now before we can go further,

11
00:00:28,530 --> 00:00:30,530
we actually need to define what amyloid is.

12
00:00:30,530 --> 00:00:35,530
So amyloid is a misfolded protein, that's important,

13
00:00:35,530 --> 00:00:39,530
that deposits in the extracellular space.

14
00:00:39,530 --> 00:00:41,530
That's actually very important as well.

15
00:00:41,530 --> 00:00:46,530
So it is a misfolded protein and it deposits outside of the cell

16
00:00:46,530 --> 00:00:48,530
in the extracellular space,

17
00:00:48,530 --> 00:00:52,530
and it's this deposition in the extracellular space

18
00:00:52,530 --> 00:00:56,530
that eventually results in damage to tissues.

19
00:00:56,530 --> 00:01:00,530
So cells become injured and the tissues become damaged.

20
00:01:00,530 --> 00:01:06,530
The next important principle concerning amyloid is that multiple proteins can deposit as amyloid.

21
00:01:06,530 --> 00:01:08,530
Amyloid is not one protein.

22
00:01:08,530 --> 00:01:19,530
Amyloid is a broad category that refers to multiple proteins depositing in a particular way.

23
00:01:19,530 --> 00:01:26,530
And there are a few common characteristics of amyloid and those are relatively important to be familiar with,

24
00:01:26,530 --> 00:01:28,530
so let's mention them here.

25
00:01:28,530 --> 00:01:35,530
First of all, the misfolded proteins that deposit as amyloid usually have a beta pleated sheet configuration.

26
00:01:35,530 --> 00:01:37,530
That's important.

27
00:01:37,530 --> 00:01:43,530
And number two, and what was the classic teaching and you're perhaps familiar with,

28
00:01:43,530 --> 00:01:51,530
is the fact that the proteins that deposit as amyloid, they pick up a stain called Congo red.

29
00:01:51,530 --> 00:01:56,530
So they'll turn red with this Congo red stain.

30
00:01:56,530 --> 00:02:03,530
And very classically, when placed under polarized light, they reveal an apple green birefringence.

31
00:02:03,530 --> 00:02:05,530
Apple green birefringence.

32
00:02:05,530 --> 00:02:08,530
So here's a couple images to highlight that.

33
00:02:08,530 --> 00:02:13,530
This is a picture of amyloid with a Congo red stain.

34
00:02:13,530 --> 00:02:15,530
Let's figure 1.13 in your text.

35
00:02:15,530 --> 00:02:19,530
And let's just focus here, what I'm boxing in here with this square.

36
00:02:19,530 --> 00:02:20,530
Let's focus here for a minute.

37
00:02:20,530 --> 00:02:27,530
First of all, notice that the center of this area here, the center of this square, sort of represents a blood vessel.

38
00:02:27,530 --> 00:02:30,530
So this circular space in the center represents a blood vessel.

39
00:02:30,530 --> 00:02:39,530
And notice that this dense pink material here, which is surrounding the blood vessel, actually represents amyloid.

40
00:02:39,530 --> 00:02:42,530
And that's what's staining red with the Congo red stain.

41
00:02:42,530 --> 00:02:44,530
Notice that the amyloid is outside of the cells.

42
00:02:44,530 --> 00:02:46,530
It's in the extracellular matrix.

43
00:02:46,530 --> 00:02:50,530
And also notice that it tends to deposit around blood vessels.

44
00:02:50,530 --> 00:02:56,530
And that's going to be very important in another subsequent discussion when we talk about Alzheimer's disease

45
00:02:56,530 --> 00:03:03,530
and the fact that in Alzheimer's disease you get amyloid plaques, but that you also get amyloid around blood vessels.

46
00:03:03,530 --> 00:03:09,530
But generally speaking, it is a principle that amyloid often deposits around blood vessels.

47
00:03:09,530 --> 00:03:19,530
This is the second stain to be familiar with, and that is when the Congo red stain is taken and it's placed under polarized light,

48
00:03:19,530 --> 00:03:26,530
it reveals this apple green birefringence, which is characteristic of amyloid.

49
00:03:26,530 --> 00:03:36,530
Now, amyloid can deposit, amyloid does deposit in tissues, and that deposition can be systemic or localized.

50
00:03:36,530 --> 00:03:38,530
Now what do we mean by systemic?

51
00:03:38,530 --> 00:03:42,530
Systemic means that it deposits in multiple organ systems of the body.

52
00:03:42,530 --> 00:03:48,530
So it's a diffuse deposition, and localized means that it's localized to a particular organ.

53
00:03:48,530 --> 00:03:53,530
Now, in systemic amyloid, we're going to focus on systemic amyloid first.

54
00:03:53,530 --> 00:04:00,530
Systemic amyloid is divided into primary and secondary types, and we're going to highlight those now.

55
00:04:00,530 --> 00:04:03,530
So what is primary amyloidosis?

56
00:04:03,530 --> 00:04:08,530
Primary amyloidosis is when there is systemic deposition of amyloid,

57
00:04:08,530 --> 00:04:17,530
and that systemic deposition of amyloid is a particular protein called AL amyloid.

58
00:04:17,530 --> 00:04:21,530
AL amyloid is derived from immunoglobulin light chain.

59
00:04:21,530 --> 00:04:27,530
So basically, immunoglobulin light chain begins to deposit in various tissues throughout the body.

60
00:04:27,530 --> 00:04:29,530
That's primary amyloidosis.

61
00:04:29,530 --> 00:04:31,530
Alright, let's take a step back.

62
00:04:31,530 --> 00:04:38,530
When you think about light chain, you should basically remember that light chain, or immunoglobulin light chain,

63
00:04:38,530 --> 00:04:39,530
is part of immunoglobulin.

64
00:04:39,530 --> 00:04:43,530
And if I draw you a picture of immunoglobulin, like this and here,

65
00:04:43,530 --> 00:04:46,530
you'll remember that there are heavy chains, as I've drawn,

66
00:04:46,530 --> 00:04:52,530
and that associated with those heavy chains are these two light chains, which are here, these light chains.

67
00:04:52,530 --> 00:04:56,530
Now generally speaking, the plasma cell, when it creates heavy chain and light chain,

68
00:04:56,530 --> 00:05:04,530
it creates it in a balance, so that the amount of heavy chain and the amount of light chain are relatively equal,

69
00:05:04,530 --> 00:05:09,530
and the heavy chain and the light chain will bind together to produce proper immunoglobulin.

70
00:05:09,530 --> 00:05:14,530
However, in a subset of diseases called plasma cell dyscrasias,

71
00:05:14,530 --> 00:05:21,530
plasma cell dyscrasias is a general term that refers to abnormalities of the plasma cell.

72
00:05:21,530 --> 00:05:27,530
When there are plasma cell dyscrasias, there can be overproduction of the light chain,

73
00:05:27,530 --> 00:05:30,530
and the overproduced light chain will leak out into the blood,

74
00:05:30,530 --> 00:05:35,530
and the light chain can then become misfolded,

75
00:05:35,530 --> 00:05:40,530
and can eventually deposit within tissues, creating a primary amyloidosis.

76
00:05:40,530 --> 00:05:44,530
So that's the characteristic of primary amyloidosis.

77
00:05:44,530 --> 00:05:49,530
And the most important thing, it's derived from immunoglobulin light chain,

78
00:05:49,530 --> 00:05:52,530
it's called AL amyloid, derived from immunoglobulin light chain,

79
00:05:52,530 --> 00:05:55,530
and it's associated with plasma cell dyscrasias.

80
00:05:55,530 --> 00:06:03,530
Secondary amyloidosis refers to systemic deposition of an amyloid called AA amyloid.

81
00:06:03,530 --> 00:06:08,530
And this AA amyloid is actually derived from a protein called SAA.

82
00:06:08,530 --> 00:06:14,530
Now, let's go back and remind ourselves that SAA, the protein SAA,

83
00:06:14,530 --> 00:06:16,530
is actually an acute phase reactant.

84
00:06:16,530 --> 00:06:20,530
So that any time there's inflammation, there's an increase in SAA,

85
00:06:20,530 --> 00:06:23,530
and the SAA can be converted to AA amyloid,

86
00:06:23,530 --> 00:06:27,530
and the AA amyloid can deposit within tissues, creating amyloidosis.

87
00:06:27,530 --> 00:06:33,530
Now, if SAA is an acute phase reactant, then we can derive from that

88
00:06:33,530 --> 00:06:37,530
that secondary amyloidosis is going to be related to circumstances

89
00:06:37,530 --> 00:06:39,530
where there is chronic inflammation.

90
00:06:39,530 --> 00:06:42,530
So for example, if the patient has a chronic inflammatory state,

91
00:06:42,530 --> 00:06:45,530
that can result in secondary amyloidosis.

92
00:06:45,530 --> 00:06:48,530
So what would be some examples of chronic inflammatory states?

93
00:06:48,530 --> 00:06:52,530
Autoimmune diseases like lupus, rheumatoid arthritis, etc.

94
00:06:52,530 --> 00:06:56,530
Another example of a chronic inflammatory state, Crohn's disease.

95
00:06:56,530 --> 00:07:00,530
An example of a chronic inflammatory state, ulcerative colitis.

96
00:07:00,530 --> 00:07:04,530
Another example of chronic inflammatory state, chronic osteomyelitis,

97
00:07:04,530 --> 00:07:08,530
in which you get a long progressive infection within the bone marrow.

98
00:07:08,530 --> 00:07:11,530
So these are all examples where there's chronic inflammation.

99
00:07:11,530 --> 00:07:14,530
Chronic inflammation results in an increase in SAA.

100
00:07:14,530 --> 00:07:18,530
SAA can become AA, and the AA can deposit as amyloid.

101
00:07:18,530 --> 00:07:23,530
Another circumstance in which we might see secondary amyloidosis is malignancy.

102
00:07:23,530 --> 00:07:29,530
And the idea here is that malignancy, on occasion, can result in a chronic inflammatory state.

103
00:07:29,530 --> 00:07:35,530
And a low-grade chronic inflammatory state because we can have an inflammatory reaction against the tumors,

104
00:07:35,530 --> 00:07:37,530
against the tumor cells.

105
00:07:37,530 --> 00:07:40,530
And so malignancy is associated with secondary amyloidosis.

106
00:07:40,530 --> 00:07:44,530
The third example, which is super rare and likely not to be seen clinically,

107
00:07:44,530 --> 00:07:49,530
except on a very rare occasion, however very commonly shows up on board exams,

108
00:07:49,530 --> 00:07:51,530
is familial Mediterranean fever.

109
00:07:51,530 --> 00:07:54,530
Now, what is familial Mediterranean fever?

110
00:07:54,530 --> 00:07:57,530
Familial Mediterranean fever is actually a dysfunction of the neutrophils.

111
00:07:57,530 --> 00:08:03,530
It is a familial genetically derived dysfunction, which is actually autosomal recessive.

112
00:08:03,530 --> 00:08:05,530
And it occurs in persons of Mediterranean origin.

113
00:08:05,530 --> 00:08:08,530
Now, what's the key consequence of familial Mediterranean fever?

114
00:08:08,530 --> 00:08:11,530
Well, the neutrophils are not functioning properly,

115
00:08:11,530 --> 00:08:17,530
and they can become activated and actually create an attack of acute inflammation.

116
00:08:17,530 --> 00:08:20,530
And that attack of acute inflammation is not really driven by infection,

117
00:08:20,530 --> 00:08:23,530
but instead is sort of this dysfunction of the neutrophils.

118
00:08:23,530 --> 00:08:29,530
And it classically presents with fever, which is what you would expect with inflammation.

119
00:08:29,530 --> 00:08:31,530
So it classically presents with fever.

120
00:08:31,530 --> 00:08:35,530
It also will usually involve serosal surfaces.

121
00:08:35,530 --> 00:08:40,530
And so if it's going to involve serosal surfaces, we'll get some very classic findings.

122
00:08:40,530 --> 00:08:44,530
For example, if it involves a serosal surface of the heart, that's the pericardium,

123
00:08:44,530 --> 00:08:48,530
it can present mimicking myocardial infarction.

124
00:08:48,530 --> 00:08:54,530
If it involves the serosal surface of the abdomen, it can present mimicking acute appendicitis.

125
00:08:54,530 --> 00:09:03,530
And so these are some circumstances, these are some of the classic clinical symptoms of familial Mediterranean fever.

126
00:09:03,530 --> 00:09:09,530
Now, what's important for this particular discussion is that when a patient has an attack,

127
00:09:09,530 --> 00:09:12,530
there's going to be a lot of inflammation.

128
00:09:12,530 --> 00:09:16,530
And a byproduct of that high inflammation will be production of acute phase reactants.

129
00:09:16,530 --> 00:09:19,530
And one of those acute phase reactants is SAA.

130
00:09:19,530 --> 00:09:25,530
And so if SAA is produced during an attack, that can deposit as AA,

131
00:09:25,530 --> 00:09:32,530
which would then obviously be AA amyloid, which can then result in amyloidosis, secondary amyloidosis.

132
00:09:32,530 --> 00:09:35,530
Now, what are the classic clinical findings?

133
00:09:35,530 --> 00:09:41,530
Essentially what you should be aware of is that amyloid can really deposit in multiple tissues throughout the body.

134
00:09:41,530 --> 00:09:46,530
However, there are some classic clinical findings, and that's what I want to emphasize here.

135
00:09:46,530 --> 00:09:50,530
First, the kidney is the most commonly involved organ, high yield.

136
00:09:50,530 --> 00:09:53,530
The kidney is the most commonly involved organ.

137
00:09:53,530 --> 00:09:59,530
And when it is involved, it usually creates nephrotic syndrome, or usually results in nephrotic syndrome.

138
00:09:59,530 --> 00:10:01,530
That's high yield as well.

139
00:10:01,530 --> 00:10:06,530
Nephrotic syndrome we will cover in much more detail when we have renal lectures,

140
00:10:06,530 --> 00:10:10,530
but basically it's a syndrome in which there is a large loss of protein,

141
00:10:10,530 --> 00:10:17,530
classically greater than 3.5 grams over a 24-hour period in the urine.

142
00:10:17,530 --> 00:10:25,530
Another region of the body that can be affected by amyloidosis or is classically affected by amyloidosis is the heart.

143
00:10:25,530 --> 00:10:27,530
And in the heart, we get a restrictive cardiomyopathy.

144
00:10:27,530 --> 00:10:29,530
What's a restrictive cardiomyopathy?

145
00:10:29,530 --> 00:10:43,530
It's a cardiomyopathy or a disease of the heart in which we can't fill the heart properly because the wall of the heart becomes less compliant or less bendable due to the filling of the wall of the heart with amyloid.

146
00:10:43,530 --> 00:10:47,530
And therefore, if we can't fill the heart well, we're not going to be able to pump well.

147
00:10:47,530 --> 00:10:51,530
If we can't pump well, we're going to get eventual heart failure.

148
00:10:51,530 --> 00:10:53,530
Another complication would be arrhythmia.

149
00:10:53,530 --> 00:10:56,530
And then finally, other organs can be involved.

150
00:10:56,530 --> 00:10:59,530
For example, the tongue will become enlarged or can become enlarged.

151
00:10:59,530 --> 00:11:02,530
The bowel can become thickened.

152
00:11:02,530 --> 00:11:05,530
The wall of the bowel can become thickened, leading to malabsorption.

153
00:11:05,530 --> 00:11:08,530
And the liver and the spleen often are enlarged as well.

154
00:11:08,530 --> 00:11:13,530
The diagnosis of amyloidosis requires a tissue biopsy.

155
00:11:13,530 --> 00:11:16,530
It's not a blood test.

156
00:11:16,530 --> 00:11:24,530
It requires you to actually see the amyloid within the tissue and, of course, perform a Congo red stain that shows apple green birefringens.

157
00:11:24,530 --> 00:11:29,530
Targets that are relatively easily accessible include the abdominal fat pad.

158
00:11:29,530 --> 00:11:32,530
So basically, it's a very simple procedure.

159
00:11:32,530 --> 00:11:38,530
A needle is just placed through the abdominal wall into the fat pad and then aspirated.

160
00:11:38,530 --> 00:11:42,530
And the cells are placed on a slide and amyloid would be visible in that way.

161
00:11:42,530 --> 00:11:46,530
The rectum is also a relatively easy target for biopsy.

162
00:11:46,530 --> 00:11:52,530
And the amyloid could be seen in the mucosa of the rectum as well.

163
00:11:52,530 --> 00:11:58,530
The damaged organs in systemic amyloidosis must be transplanted.

164
00:11:58,530 --> 00:12:03,530
If you get end-stage damage, amyloid cannot be removed.

165
00:12:03,530 --> 00:12:07,530
And so that's an important clinical principle concerning amyloidosis.

166
00:12:07,530 --> 00:12:11,530
Now, that was the discussion of systemic amyloidosis.

167
00:12:11,530 --> 00:12:14,530
And now I want to talk about localized amyloidosis.

168
00:12:14,530 --> 00:12:31,530
And in the discussion of localized amyloidosis, the first point here is that the word localized basically refers to the fact that the amyloid deposition is localized to a single organ, usually a single organ or a single tissue in the body.

169
00:12:31,530 --> 00:12:37,530
The first and classic example of localized amyloidosis is senile cardiac amyloidosis.

170
00:12:37,530 --> 00:12:38,530
So the name kind of gives it away.

171
00:12:38,530 --> 00:12:40,530
Senile, usually seen in the elderly.

172
00:12:40,530 --> 00:12:43,530
Cardiac, usually involves the heart alone.

173
00:12:43,530 --> 00:12:47,530
And of course, amyloidosis means that there's an amyloid depositing there.

174
00:12:47,530 --> 00:12:53,530
It is important for examination purposes to know what the amyloid is in this particular circumstance.

175
00:12:53,530 --> 00:12:57,530
It is non-mutated serum transthyretin.

176
00:12:57,530 --> 00:12:59,530
So we could just basically say this.

177
00:12:59,530 --> 00:13:00,530
It is serum transthyretin.

178
00:13:00,530 --> 00:13:04,530
And you'll see why I'm highlighting the non-mutated part in a moment.

179
00:13:04,530 --> 00:13:10,530
So serum transthyretin is the second most common protein within the blood.

180
00:13:10,530 --> 00:13:19,530
And this protein, over very long periods of time, can eventually deposit, particularly in the heart, resulting in senile cardiac amyloidosis.

181
00:13:19,530 --> 00:13:21,530
This would be asymptomatic.

182
00:13:21,530 --> 00:13:22,530
That's important.

183
00:13:22,530 --> 00:13:24,530
And it would be seen in the elderly.

184
00:13:24,530 --> 00:13:31,530
In fact, it's relatively common, present in about 25% of individuals over the age of 80.

185
00:13:31,530 --> 00:13:41,530
Another localized amyloidosis, which is sort of in contrast to senile cardiac amyloidosis, is familial amyloid cardiomyopathy.

186
00:13:41,530 --> 00:13:45,530
Now, in this particular case, it's serum transthyretin that deposits.

187
00:13:45,530 --> 00:13:53,530
However, it is a mutated, not a normal, but a mutated serum transthyretin that deposits in the heart.

188
00:13:53,530 --> 00:13:56,530
This also results in disease.

189
00:13:56,530 --> 00:14:00,530
Remember that in senile cardiac amyloidosis, it was asymptomatic.

190
00:14:00,530 --> 00:14:04,530
But here, this leads to a restrictive cardiomyopathy.

191
00:14:04,530 --> 00:14:10,530
Again, restrictive cardiomyopathy implying that the wall of the heart gets full of amyloid.

192
00:14:10,530 --> 00:14:12,530
Because it's full of amyloid, it's not able to expand.

193
00:14:12,530 --> 00:14:15,530
It becomes less compliant, and therefore it can't fill.

194
00:14:15,530 --> 00:14:17,530
So it's restricted from filling.

195
00:14:17,530 --> 00:14:21,530
And therefore, as a consequence of that, it can't pump properly.

196
00:14:21,530 --> 00:14:26,530
And if it can't pump properly, eventually the patient goes into cardiac failure.

197
00:14:26,530 --> 00:14:29,530
This is a familial circumstance.

198
00:14:29,530 --> 00:14:33,530
5% of African Americans actually carry the mutated gene.

199
00:14:33,530 --> 00:14:38,530
So those are the key highlights for familial amyloid cardiomyopathy.

200
00:14:38,530 --> 00:14:43,530
The next of the examples of localized amyloidosis is type 2 diabetes.

201
00:14:43,530 --> 00:14:53,530
In type 2 diabetes, recall that the problem is a resistance to insulin at the level of the skeletal muscle and the adipose tissue.

202
00:14:53,530 --> 00:15:03,530
And that resistance to insulin initially results in the pancreas actually putting out excess insulin until eventually the pancreas burns out.

203
00:15:03,530 --> 00:15:13,530
And so, a consequence of the production of that excess insulin is actually the production of a byproduct of insulin, which is called amylin.

204
00:15:13,530 --> 00:15:16,530
And amylin deposits in the islets of the pancreas.

205
00:15:16,530 --> 00:15:23,530
So the classic histologic finding in type 2 diabetes is deposition of amyloid within the islets of the pancreas.

206
00:15:23,530 --> 00:15:27,530
And that amyloid is called amylin, which is again derived from insulin.

207
00:15:27,530 --> 00:15:31,900
Another example, Alzheimer's disease.

208
00:15:31,900 --> 00:15:38,900
In Alzheimer's disease, there is deposition of amyloid within the brain as plaques.

209
00:15:38,900 --> 00:15:44,900
Those plaques are actually made up of an amyloid called A-beta amyloid.

210
00:15:44,900 --> 00:15:46,900
It's important to know A-beta amyloid.

211
00:15:46,900 --> 00:15:52,900
A-beta amyloid is derived from a protein called the beta amyloid precursor protein.

212
00:15:52,900 --> 00:15:55,900
Beta amyloid precursor protein.

213
00:15:55,900 --> 00:15:59,900
So, in Alzheimer's disease, we see amyloid plaques.

214
00:15:59,900 --> 00:16:02,900
The amyloid plaques contain A-beta amyloid.

215
00:16:02,900 --> 00:16:07,900
And the A-beta amyloid is actually derived from beta amyloid precursor protein.

216
00:16:07,900 --> 00:16:12,900
Important to know and relatively high yield is that the beta amyloid precursor protein,

217
00:16:12,900 --> 00:16:15,900
the gene that produces that protein, is on chromosome 21.

218
00:16:15,900 --> 00:16:17,900
Why is that important?

219
00:16:17,900 --> 00:16:24,900
It's important because it ties into a very important clinical high yield association.

220
00:16:24,900 --> 00:16:32,900
And that is that most individuals with Down syndrome, i.e. individuals who have three copies of chromosome 21,

221
00:16:32,900 --> 00:16:35,900
actually develop Alzheimer's disease by the age of 40.

222
00:16:35,900 --> 00:16:38,900
And that's an example of early onset Alzheimer's disease.

223
00:16:38,900 --> 00:16:45,900
By early onset, what we mean is that the patient gets Alzheimer's disease at an age earlier than would normally be expected.

224
00:16:45,900 --> 00:16:51,900
An additional type of localized amyloidosis is dialysis-associated amyloidosis.

225
00:16:51,900 --> 00:16:54,900
A few very high yield principles here.

226
00:16:54,900 --> 00:16:58,900
Number one, the amyloid that deposits is called beta-2 microglobulin.

227
00:16:58,900 --> 00:17:01,900
Number two, it deposits in joints.

228
00:17:01,900 --> 00:17:05,900
And number three, it occurs in patients who are on dialysis.

229
00:17:05,900 --> 00:17:08,900
So, let's take a step back and just review one quick principle.

230
00:17:08,900 --> 00:17:11,900
And that is, what is beta-2 microglobulin?

231
00:17:11,900 --> 00:17:20,900
So, recall that one of the surface molecules present on all nucleated cells and platelets is MHC class I.

232
00:17:20,900 --> 00:17:26,900
An MHC class I has a transmembrane domain and an extracellular domain.

233
00:17:26,900 --> 00:17:33,900
An important molecule that provides structural support for MHC class I is called beta-2 microglobulin.

234
00:17:33,900 --> 00:17:35,900
So, beta-2 microglobulin.

235
00:17:35,900 --> 00:17:42,900
Without beta-2 microglobulin, MHC class I is not well expressed on the surface of cells.

236
00:17:42,900 --> 00:17:47,900
Now, patients who get kidney failure and have to go on dialysis.

237
00:17:47,900 --> 00:17:52,900
When they go on dialysis, the beta-2 microglobulin is not filtered well from the blood.

238
00:17:52,900 --> 00:17:55,900
And so, because it's not filtered well, it builds up in the blood.

239
00:17:55,900 --> 00:17:59,900
And as it builds up in the blood, it can deposit in joints.

240
00:17:59,900 --> 00:18:04,900
And these are the classic high yields of this particular entity.

241
00:18:04,900 --> 00:18:06,900
Again, it's dialysis associated.

242
00:18:06,900 --> 00:18:09,900
The deposition is beta-2 microglobulin.

243
00:18:09,900 --> 00:18:14,900
Beta-2 microglobulin is a component, a structural component, of MHC class I.

244
00:18:14,900 --> 00:18:21,900
And this beta-2 microglobulin will deposit in joints as a localized amyloidosis.

245
00:18:21,900 --> 00:18:28,900
And the final localized amyloidosis arises in medullary carcinoma of the thyroid.

246
00:18:28,900 --> 00:18:35,900
Recall that medullary carcinoma of the thyroid is a tumor of the thyroid,

247
00:18:35,900 --> 00:18:41,900
and that tumor is a tumor that's derived from C cells.

248
00:18:41,900 --> 00:18:48,900
And C cells are neuroendocrine-derived cells present within the thyroid

249
00:18:48,900 --> 00:18:52,900
that actually produce a protein called calcitonin.

250
00:18:52,900 --> 00:18:58,900
Now, if there is a tumor of C cells, then there is overproduction of calcitonin,

251
00:18:58,900 --> 00:19:04,900
and part of that excess calcitonin can deposit within the tumor,

252
00:19:04,900 --> 00:19:13,900
creating amyloid in association with medullary carcinoma of the thyroid.

253
00:19:13,900 --> 00:19:20,900
And the classic histologic description is that there are tumor cells in an amyloid background.

254
00:19:20,900 --> 00:19:25,900
And this can show up on examinations where they tell you that a patient has a thyroid mask,

255
00:19:25,900 --> 00:19:30,900
a fine needle aspiration is performed, i.e., a needle is stuck into the thyroid and cells are pulled away.

256
00:19:30,900 --> 00:19:32,900
That's the way by which we biopsy the thyroid.

257
00:19:32,900 --> 00:19:39,900
By the way, that's an important principle, i.e., that the thyroid is biopsied by fine needle aspiration.

258
00:19:39,900 --> 00:19:42,900
So the needle is placed in the tumor, the cells are pulled away,

259
00:19:42,900 --> 00:19:47,900
and they tell you that the pathologist sees tumor cells in an amyloid background.

260
00:19:47,900 --> 00:19:52,900
That tells you that you're dealing with medullary carcinoma, especially on an examination.

261
00:19:52,900 --> 00:19:57,900
And that really is the last of the localized amyloidosis, and that is the last of chapter 1.

262
00:19:57,900 --> 00:19:58,900
End of chapter 1

263
00:19:58,900 --> 00:20:00,900
End of chapter 1

264
00:20:00,900 --> 00:20:21,900
End of chapter 1

